From c0da4394e08b22d590bd4e3fb7a1b5d6de8d5737 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gerontec <gh@heissa.de>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:16:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] makedb: add --append to add sequences to an existing
 database

Rebuilding a large database from scratch in order to add one day of new
sequences is expensive: for NCBI nr a full makedb run takes hours, while the
daily delta is a few hundred thousand sequences.

--append reads the header of the existing database, truncates the file at
pos_array_offset and continues writing sequences there. When the trailer is
written, the old offset table is copied back first and the new entries follow;
the same is done for the taxon id lists, and TaxonList::build() is called with
the number of newly added sequences only.

Everything from pos_array_offset to EOF is copied to <database>.append_backup
before the database is truncated. If the append fails at any point, or if the
backup file already exists from an earlier interrupted run, the database is
restored to its previous state.

The taxonomy options have to be used exactly as in the original build:
--taxonmap if and only if the database contains taxon id lists, and likewise
for --taxonnodes and --taxonnames. Protein databases only.

Verified on a 2.5 million sequence subset of nr: building it in two steps with
--append yields a .dmnd that is byte-identical to the single-step build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
